cellSpurs: run a policy module that has no lift in the SPU interpreter, and give the instance's SPUs thread ids and SNRs.

Game: A Crack in Time 1.20 (run acit-boot4): the second SPURS instance "ig" (nSpus 5) has one workload, wid 2 "igPm/igPmWorkload",
policy module 0x00AD9080, 0x230 bytes, argument 0x00FBB240. It had no lift, so spurs_resolve_pm refused it ("PM NOT LIFTED --
workload will not run"); its ready count stayed 5 ([spurs-inst] "ig" ready=..05.. enabled=20000000) and the main thread spun
forever at 0x0055CA4C (func_0055C828, called from 0x004E8950) waiting for bit 0x100 of the halfword at [[r30-0x7FB4]] + 0x0C,
which Insomniac's SPU job kernel sets; last flip 6.66 s.
The policy module is a loader (ila x4 header, entry LS 0xA00): it reads SPU_RdSigNotify1 as its SPU index, GETs 48 bytes at the
workload argument, GETs the job kernel (EA 0x00AD8B00, 0x510 bytes, entry word +0x18 = 8 -> LS 0x3F5A0) to LS 0x3F580 and bisl's
to it. The kernel never returns: a getllar/putllc ring of 8 job slots at LS 0x3FE00 (lock-line-lost events, mask 0x400) that
DMAs each job's header and code from main memory and calls it. The game writes SNR1 = i to SPU i of the instance with
sys_spu_thread_write_snr (@0x0069546C) on the ids cellSpursGetInfo returned (info at r1+0xB0, spuThreads[] read at r1+0xD8 =
info + 0x28); GetInfo wrote 0 there, so the write failed.

Changes:
 - spurs_resolve_pm: a policy module with no lift gets spurs_pm_interp_entry (spurs_policy.c: spu_interp_run from LS 0xA00),
   image id -1 = pure interpretation (never rejoins another image's lifted functions). RC_SPURS_PM_INTERP=0 = the old refusal.
 - cellSpursGetInfo: spuThreads[i] (+0x28) = 0x7E000000 | instance << 8 | i for i < nSpus (synthetic: the instance's SPUs are
   kernel threads, not lv2 SPU threads; ASSUMPTION: no title compares them with ids from elsewhere).
 - sys_spu_thread_write_snr: an id lv2 does not know goes to g_spurs_snr_hook (installed by spurs_initialize_common), which
   writes the SNR of that (instance, SPU): spurs_pm_snr_write keeps one register per (instance, SPU) (overwrite, 1 deep); the
   policy-module context running on that SPU gets it at once (and is woken), else it is pending and the next run on that SPU
   takes it into its context; what a run did not read is given back when it ends.
ToD: every policy module of ToD is lifted (no change), it never calls write_snr (no syscall 184 in its lifted code), and an id
from GetInfo passed to any other SPU-thread syscall fails with ESRCH as 0 did.
Host test: port/rc/tests/run_spurs_pm_interp_test.sh.

